      "writtenOn": "2024-10-30T18:18:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Should we have reserved protection-id for unprotected buffers as well? Do we need a separate protection id for encoded and decoded secure video buffers?",
      "revId": "e1445667a76c9ae1b12e14d8330c325563c0d4f3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "649d3b89_8c3d5bc5",
        "filename": "staging/security/see/hwcrypto/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/hwcrypto/types/ProtectionId.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 27,
      "author": {
        "id": 1942645
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-31T00:06:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Right now if a ProtectionID is not used, then the buffer is an unprotected one. Would you prefer this to be explicit? One thing I don\u0027t like too much about the explicit enum value is that it makes possible for a caller to try to set up a ProtectionID like SECURE_VIDEO_BUFFER and also try to set it to the unprotected enum value.\nOn the API [the function call to set up protection IDs here](https://android-review.git.corp.google.com/c/platform/hardware/interfaces/+/3122114/4/staging/security/see/hwcrypto/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/hwcrypto/IOpaqueKey.aidl#89) distinguish between a buffer from which we read and the one we write to. This should cover the encoded/decoded case, wouldn\u0027t it?",
      "parentUuid": "97541183_4fcd09f4",
      "revId": "e1445667a76c9ae1b12e14d8330c325563c0d4f3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ba20140b_c5057804",
        "filename": "staging/security/see/hwcrypto/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/hwcrypto/types/ProtectionId.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 27,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-08T00:45:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We have places where a 0 protection id value means unprotected. If this enum value is local to the API that sets a protection id, then I understand not wanting to allow an unprotected value, but if we want to use the same value in other APIs that return a protection ID, then having a way to represent unprotected buffers might be needed.\n\nRegarding the encoded/decoded case the flow I\u0027m referring to is:\nunprotected-encrypted-encoded-buffer \u003d\u003e protected-encoded-buffer \u003d\u003e protected-unencoded-buffer.\n\nhwcrypto does not need to touch the last one, but the decoder needs to read a buffer with the protection-id you defined here and write to the protected-unencoded-buffer. protected-encoded-buffer and protected-unencoded-buffer could use the same id and even decode in place, but if the these are separate buffers then it means the encoder will have write access to buffers it does not need to write to and hwcrypto will have write access to buffer it does not need access to at all.",
      "parentUuid": "649d3b89_8c3d5bc5",
      "revId": "e1445667a76c9ae1b12e14d8330c325563c0d4f3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "696e54e1_22897b0d",
        "filename": "staging/security/see/hwcrypto/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/hwcrypto/types/ProtectionId.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 27,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-08T18:08:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I have an additional question here. What happens when we want to update this list in the next stable version of this interface. Can we add values here and still use the setProtectionId we defined in this version of the API, or do we need to add a new setProtectionId function that takes the version 2 ProtectionId enum type as an argument?",
      "parentUuid": "ba20140b_c5057804",
      "revId": "e1445667a76c9ae1b12e14d8330c325563c0d4f3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
